!!id:"https://schemas.example.com/2026/36/experiment/meta-service/orders-api-inline-1.tn"
!!meta:"https://tson.io/2026/36/ltr8/http/meta-service-1.tn"
!!import:"https://schemas.example.com/2026/36/experiment/meta-service/orders-types-1.tn"
!!import:"https://schemas.example.com/2026/36/experiment/meta-service/orders-errors-1.tn"
@doc:"""
  Web service only: an api on its own terms, with no interface behind it, so every endpoint is an
  `!operation` carrying its signature inline. The verb and the path are the keys an endpoint sits under;
  a path key is data, so `/orders/{id}` needs no identifier minted from it. A parameter-only endpoint is a
  one-field request record placed from the path.

  A method's facts ride on its own key, so on an inline operation they sit before the verb: a GET must be
  `@safe`, and the reader refuses one that is not.

  This example used to serve every schema at its identity's path, which is a real endpoint of this project's
  demos and the one thing this vocabulary cannot describe -- its 200 carries bytes rather than TSON, and
  `signature.response` names a type. It was replaced rather than fudged; see the README's absent list.
  """
{
  orders_api => !api {
    "/orders" => !resource {
      @doc:"Place an order. The whole body is the order."
      POST => !operation { request: order  response: order  errors: [sku_not_found]  status: 201 }
    }
    "/orders/{id}" => !resource {
      @safe
      @doc:"One order, by the id in the path."
      GET => !operation { request: order_ref  response: order  errors: [order_not_found] }
    }
    "/orders/{id}/items" => !resource {
      @safe
      @doc:"""
        The lines of one order: a second parameter-only endpoint, and a nested path written as a template of
        its own -- the URL namespace is hierarchical and a resource key is flat over it, which is the
        qualified-name question wearing a slash.
        """
      GET => !operation { request: order_ref  response: order_page }
    }
  }
}
